# Life Expectancy Prediction Using Machine Learning

## Project Overview

This project develops a **Linear Regression** model to predict a country's life expectancy using demographic, economic, healthcare, and mortality indicators.

The project demonstrates the complete machine learning workflow, including data cleaning, exploratory data analysis (EDA), feature engineering, preprocessing, model training, evaluation, and regression diagnostics.

---

##  Dataset

The dataset contains country-level health and socioeconomic indicators collected over multiple years.

### Target Variable

- **Life expectancy**

### Features include

- Adult Mortality
- Infant deaths
- Alcohol Consumption
- Percentage Expenditure
- Hepatitis B
- Measles
- BMI
- Under-five deaths
- Polio
- Total Expenditure
- Diphtheria
- HIV/AIDS
- GDP
- Population
- Thinness (1–19 years)
- Thinness (5–9 years)
- Income Composition of Resources
- Schooling
- Status (Developed / Developing)
- Year

---

# Project Objectives

- Explore factors influencing life expectancy.
- Perform exploratory data analysis.
- Clean and preprocess the dataset.
- Handle missing values and outliers.
- Engineer meaningful features.
- Train a Linear Regression model.
- Evaluate model performance using multiple regression metrics.
- Validate regression assumptions through residual analysis.

---

# Technologies Used

- Python
- Pandas
- NumPy
- Matplotlib
- Seaborn
- Scikit-learn
- SciPy

---

# Pipeline Summary
Missing value imputation — median by country, with Status-level and global fallbacks; rows with missing target values dropped.
Categorical encoding — dummy encoding for Status; Country dropped due to high cardinality.
Outlier handling — log-transform applied to naturally skewed columns (GDP, Population, HIV/AIDS, etc.) rather than removing genuine extreme values.
Train-test split — 80/20, performed before scaling or feature selection to prevent data leakage.
Feature selection — correlation analysis on the training set; multicollinear and weakly-correlated features removed, leaving 14 features.
Scaling — StandardScaler, fit on training data only.
Model — LinearRegression (scikit-learn).
Evaluation — R², RMSE, MAE, plus Q-Q and residual plots.

---

# Data Preprocessing
## Missing Value Treatment
Target column (Life expectancy) and Adult Mortality: 10 rows had missing values, all from countries with only a single row in the dataset (e.g., Monaco, Nauru, San Marino). Since no country-level average could be computed for these, and imputing the target risks fabricating information the model would learn from, these 10 rows were dropped.
All other columns with missing values (Hepatitis B, GDP, Population, Alcohol, BMI, Polio, Total expenditure, Diphtheria, thinness indicators, Income composition of resources, Schooling): imputed using a three-tier median strategy — country median first, falling back to Developed/Developing status median where a country had no data at all, with a global median as a final safety net.
## Categorical Encoding
Status (Developed/Developing): dummy encoded into a single binary column (Status_Developing), avoiding the dummy variable trap.
Country (193 unique values): dropped as a model feature due to high cardinality relative to sample size; its information is substantially captured by GDP, Income composition, Schooling, and Population instead.
## Outlier Handling

Boxplot analysis identified several columns with a high number of statistical outliers (HIV/AIDS, Measles, GDP, Population, under-five deaths, percentage expenditure, infant deaths). These were determined to be genuine right-skew in real-world data (e.g., large countries naturally have larger populations) rather than data errors, so a log transform (log1p) was applied instead of removing the values, preserving all rows while correcting the skew.

## Train-Test Split

Data was split 80/20 into training (2,342 rows) and test (586 rows) sets before any scaling or feature-selection statistics were computed, to prevent test-set information from leaking into modeling decisions.

## Feature Selection

A correlation analysis (Pearson) was run on the training set only. Two pairs of severely multicollinear features were identified and reduced to one each:

infant deaths vs. under-five deaths (r = 0.997) → kept under-five deaths
thinness 1-19 years vs. thinness 5-9 years (r = 0.93) → kept thinness 1-19 years

Population, Year, and Total expenditure were dropped for showing weak or negligible correlation with the target (|r| < 0.23).

Final feature set (14 features): Schooling, Income composition of resources, Adult Mortality, HIV/AIDS, GDP, BMI, under-five deaths, thinness 1-19 years, Status_Developing, Diphtheria, Polio, Alcohol, percentage expenditure, Hepatitis B, Measles.

## Feature Scaling

Features were standardized (StandardScaler) — fit on the training set only, then applied to both training and test sets, to avoid data leakage.

# Model

A standard Linear Regression model was fit on the scaled training data.

# Model Performance

| Metric | Score |
|---------|-------|
| R² Score | **0.85** |
| RMSE | **3.57** |
| MAE | **2.76** |

### Interpretation

The model explains approximately **85% of the variation in life expectancy**.

On average, predictions differ from the true life expectancy by approximately **2.76 years**.

---

# Regression Diagnostics

To validate the assumptions of Linear Regression, residual analysis was performed.

Diagnostics included:

- Residual vs Predicted Plot
- Q-Q Plot
- Residual Distribution

### Findings

- Residuals were randomly distributed around zero.
- No obvious systematic pattern was observed.
- Residuals were approximately normally distributed.
- Slight deviations in the tails suggest a few extreme prediction errors, which are expected in real-world datasets.

Overall, the regression assumptions were reasonably satisfied.

