# Future Improvements

-Heavy-tailed residuals suggest a handful of countries/years are poorly captured by the linear relationship
 possibly due to factors not present in this dataset (conflict, healthcare system quality, pandemics).
-The mild heteroscedasticity in the mid-prediction range suggests model precision is not perfectly uniform
 across the full life expectancy range.
-Median imputation, while simple and appropriate for a first project, slightly reduces variance in the imputed
 columns compared to more advanced methods (e.g., KNN or regression imputation).

---

# Conclusion 
The linear regression model provides a solid, interpretable baseline for predicting life expectancy, 
explaining 85% of variance with an average error of under 3 years. Socioeconomic and health-system factors 
(schooling, income composition, adult mortality, HIV/AIDS prevalence) emerged as the dominant predictors, 
consistent with established public health research.

